{
  "image_name": "bd4dc1b5dbbfcf5abfd48ae87af336f3.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure explains how a transistor made from a two-dimensional crystal channel operates, linking device structure, band diagrams, carrier momentum, and current–voltage behavior. At the top left, a gate is separated from the 2D channel by an insulator, with source and drain contacts and applied gate and drain biases. The top-right sketches show that the gate voltage shifts the channel bands and controls the barrier relative to the source Fermi level, while drain bias lowers the energy on the drain side.\n\nThe lower panels illustrate how increasing gate overdrive turns the device on by filling more electronic states in the channel. In momentum-space drawings, the occupied states expand as gate voltage rises, corresponding to a larger current on the transfer curve. Under large drain bias, the drain-side potential drops enough that only right-moving carriers injected from the source remain available, indicating current saturation. At lower drain bias, both right-going carriers from the source and left-going carriers from the drain contribute, so net current is smaller.\n\nOverall, the figure presents a ballistic or near-ballistic picture of transistor transport in a 2D channel: gate voltage sets carrier population and threshold behavior, while drain voltage drives current and eventually leads to saturation.",
